{"id": "section-body-21475", "title": "Heart Failure", "chapterId": "chapter-twenty", "blocks": [{"id": "body-21475", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading2", "text": "Heart Failure", "runs": [{"text": "Heart"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Failure"}], "level": 2, "list": {"level": 1, "numberingId": "781"}}, {"id": "body-21476", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Heart Failure is a clinical syndrome characterized by typical symptoms (e.g. breathlessness, ankle swelling and fatigue) that may be accompanied by signs (e.g. elevated jugular venous pressure, pulmonary crackles and peripheral oedema) caused by a structural and/or functional cardiac abnormality, resulting in a reduced cardiac output and/or elevated intracardiac pressures at rest or during stress.", "runs": [{"text": "Heart Failure is a clinical syndrome characterized by typical symptoms (e.g. breathlessness, ankle swelling and fatigue) that may"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "be"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "accompanied"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "by"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "signs"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(e.g."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "elevated"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "jugular"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "venous"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "pressure,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "pulmonary"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "crackles"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "peripheral"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "oedema)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "caused"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "by a"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "structural"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and/or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "functional"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "cardiac"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "abnormality,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "resulting"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "a"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "reduced"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "cardiac"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "output"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and/or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "elevated"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "intracardiac"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "pressures at rest or during stress."}]}, {"id": "body-21478", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Heart Failure in Pregnancy", "runs": [{"text": "Heart"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Failure"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Pregnancy"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 2, "numberingId": "781"}}, {"id": "body-21479", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Symptoms and signs of heart failure in a pregnant woman are an indication for urgent assessment to establish a diagnosis and appropriate management. This is best accomplished through a multidisciplinary approach in which both cardiologists and obstetricians need to participate in order to provide expert counselling and care in pursuit of safe motherhood, as well as to exclude underlying cause of heart failure.", "runs": [{"text": "Symptoms"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "signs"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "heart"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "failure"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "a"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "pregnant"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "woman"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "are"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "an"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "indication"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "for"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "urgent"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "assessment"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "to"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "establish"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "a"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "diagnosis"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and appropriate management. This is best accomplished through a multidisciplinary approach in which both cardiologists and obstetricians need to participate in order to provide expert counselling and care in pursuit of safe motherhood, as well as to exclude underlying cause of heart failure."}]}, {"id": "body-21481", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Acute Heart Failure (AHF) or Decompensated Acute Heart Failure (ADHF)", "runs": [{"text": "Acute"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Heart"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Failure"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(AHF)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Decompensated"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Acute"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Heart"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Failure"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(ADHF)"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 2, "numberingId": "781"}}, {"id": "body-21482", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "AHF is defined as rapid or gradual onset of signs and symptoms of heart failure that results in urgent unplanned hospitalization or Emergency Medicine Department visits. The clinical signs and symptoms are significantly life threatening if the above features occur in patients with established diagnosis with structurally heart disease categorized as Acute Decompensated Heart Failure (ADHF). The cause and immediate precipitating factor(s) of the AHF must be identified and treated to prevent further damage to the heart.", "runs": [{"text": "AHF"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "defined"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "as"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "rapid"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "gradual"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "onset"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "signs"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "symptoms"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "heart"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "failure"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "that"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "results"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "urgent"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "unplanned"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "hospitalization or Emergency Medicine Department visits. The clinical signs and symptoms are significantly life threatening if the above features"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "occur"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "patients"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "with"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "established"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "diagnosis"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "with"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "structurally"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "heart"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "disease"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "categorized"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "as"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Acute"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Decompensated"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Heart Failure (ADHF). The cause and immediate precipitating factor(s) of the AHF must be identified and treated to prevent further damage to the heart."}]}, {"id": "body-21484", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Non-Pharmacological Management", "runs": [{"text": "Non-Pharmacological"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Management"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-21485", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Oxygen therapy and/or ventilatory support.", "runs": [{"text": "Oxygen"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "therapy"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and/or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "ventilatory"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "support."}]}, {"id": "body-21486", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Non-invasive positive pressure ventilation includes both CPAP and bi–level positive pressure ventilation (PPV)", "runs": [{"text": "Non-invasive"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "positive"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "pressure"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "ventilation"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "includes"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "both"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "CPAP"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "bi–level"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "positive"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "pressure"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "ventilation"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(PPV)"}], "list": {"level": 0, "numberingId": "687"}}, {"id": "body-21487", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Mechanical ventilation", "runs": [{"text": "Mechanical"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "ventilation"}], "list": {"level": 0, "numberingId": "687"}}, {"id": "body-21488-textbox1-00001", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Note: In AHF, oxygen should not be used routinely in non–hypoxemic patients, as it causes vasoconstriction and a reduction in cardiac output", "runs": [{"text": "Note:", "bold": true}, {"text": " ", "bold": true}, {"text": "In"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "AHF,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "oxygen"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "should"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "not"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "be"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "used"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "routinely"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "non–hypoxemic"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "patients,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "as"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "it"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "causes"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "vasoconstriction"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "a"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "reduction in cardiac output"}]}, {"id": "body-21490", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Pharmacological Management", "runs": [{"text": "Pharmacological"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Management"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-21491", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Recommendations for the management of patients with acute heart failure:", "runs": [{"text": "Recommendations"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "for"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "management"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "patients"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "with"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "acute"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "heart"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "failure:"}]}, {"id": "body-21493", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Diuretics: Diuretics are a cornerstone in the treatment of patients with AHF. In patients with new- onset AHF or those with chronic, decompensated HF not receiving oral diuretics the initial recommended dose should be 20–40 mg intravenous furosemide (or equivalent); for those on chronic diuretic therapy, initial intravenous dose should be at least equivalent to oral dose. Diuretics should either be given as intermittent boluses or as a continuous infusion, and the dose and duration should be adjusted according to patients’ symptoms and clinical status. Combination of loop diuretic with either thiazide-type diuretic or spironolactone may be considered", "runs": [{"text": "Diuretics: ", "bold": true}, {"text": "Diuretics are a cornerstone in the treatment of patients with AHF. In patients with new- onset AHF or those with chronic, decompensated HF not receiving oral diuretics the initial recommended dose should be 20–40 mg intravenous furosemide (or equivalent); for those on chronic diuretic therapy, initial intravenous dose should be at least equivalent to oral dose."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Diuretics"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "should"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "either"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "be"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "given"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "as"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "intermittent"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "boluses"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "as"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "a"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "continuous"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "infusion,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "dose"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "duration"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "should"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "be adjusted according to patients’ symptoms and clinical status. Combination of loop diuretic with either thiazide-type diuretic or spironolactone may be considered"}]}, {"id": "body-21494", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Loop diuretic", "runs": [{"text": "Loop"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "diuretic"}]}, {"id": "body-21495", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "A: furosemide (IV) 20–120mg 24hourly Children", "runs": [{"text": "A:", "bold": true}, {"text": " ", "bold": true}, {"text": "furosemide"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(IV)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "20–120mg"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "24hourly "}, {"text": "Children"}]}, {"id": "body-21496", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "IM/IV Bolus: 1-2mg/kg/dose every 6 – 8 hours, can be increased by 1-2mg/kg/dose every 6-8 hours (maximum dose 6mg/kg/dose)", "runs": [{"text": "IM/IV Bolus: 1-2mg/kg/dose every 6 – 8 hours, can be increased by 1-2mg/kg/dose every 6-8 hours (maximum dose "}, {"text": "6mg/kg/dose)"}], "list": {"level": 0, "numberingId": "687"}}, {"id": "body-21497", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "IV Infusion: 0.1-0.4mg/kg/h", "runs": [{"text": "IV"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Infusion:"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "0.1-"}, {"text": "0.4mg/kg/h"}], "list": {"level": 0, "numberingId": "687"}}, {"id": "body-21498", "type": "paragraph", "style": "Heading3", "text": "OR", "runs": [{"text": "OR"}], "level": 3, "conjunction": true}, {"id": "body-21499", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "S: torsemide (PO) 5–20mg 24hourly", "runs": [{"text": "S:", "bold": true}, {"text": " ", "bold": true}, {"text": "torsemide"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(PO)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "5–20mg"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "24hourly"}]}, {"id": "body-21500", "type": "paragraph", "style": "Heading3", "text": "AND", "runs": [{"text": "AND"}], "level": 3, "conjunction": true}, {"id": "body-21501", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Mineralocorticoid (Aldosterone) Receptor Antagonists:", "runs": [{"text": "Mineralocorticoid"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(Aldosterone)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Receptor"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Antagonists:"}]}, {"id": "body-21502", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "C: spironolactone (PO) 25–50mg 24hourly Children", "runs": [{"text": "C:", "bold": true}, {"text": " ", "bold": true}, {"text": "spironolactone"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(PO)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "25–50mg"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "24hourly "}, {"text": "Children"}]}, {"id": "body-21503", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "PO: 1-3mg/kg/day divided 12hourly", "runs": [{"text": "PO:"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "1-3mg/kg/day"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "divided"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "12hourly"}]}, {"id": "body-21504", "type": "paragraph", "style": "Heading3", "text": "OR", "runs": [{"text": "OR"}], "level": 3, "conjunction": true}, {"id": "body-21505", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "S: eplerenone (PO) 25–50mg 24hourly", "runs": [{"text": "S:", "bold": true}, {"text": " ", "bold": true}, {"text": "eplerenone"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(PO)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "25–50mg"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "24hourly"}]}, {"id": "body-21507", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Vasodilators", "runs": [{"text": "Vasodilators"}], "level": 4}, {"id": "body-21508", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "These are the cornerstone of treatment of AHF and have dual benefit by decreasing venous tone (to optimize preload) and arterial tone (decrease afterload). Consequently, they increase stroke volume.", "runs": [{"text": "These are the cornerstone of treatment of AHF and have dual benefit by decreasing venous tone (to optimize preload) and arterial tone (decrease afterload). Consequently, they increase stroke volume."}]}, {"id": "body-21509", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Intravenous vasodilators should be considered for symptomatic relief in AHF with SBP >90 mmHg (and without symptomatic hypotension). Symptoms and blood pressure should be monitored frequently during administration of intravenous vasodilators. In patients with hypertensive AHF, intravenous vasodilators should be considered as initial therapy to improve symptoms and reduce congestion. Intravenous vasodilators for treating AHF are described in table 20.4.", "runs": [{"text": "Intravenous vasodilators should be considered for symptomatic relief in AHF with SBP >90 mmHg (and without symptomatic hypotension)."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Symptoms"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "blood"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "pressure"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "should"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "be"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "monitored"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "frequently"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "during"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "administration"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "intravenous"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "vasodilators. In patients with hypertensive AHF, intravenous vasodilators should be considered as initial therapy to improve symptoms and reduce congestion. Intravenous vasodilators for treating AHF are described in table 20.4."}]}, {"id": "body-21510-textbox1-00001", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Note: Vasodilators should be used with caution in patients with significant mitral or aortic stenosis.", "runs": [{"text": "Note:", "bold": true}, {"text": " ", "bold": true}, {"text": "Vasodilators"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "should"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "be"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "used"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "with"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "caution"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "patients"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "with"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "significant"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "mitral"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "aortic"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "stenosis."}]}, {"id": "body-21511", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Consider oral vasodilators in case intravenous vasodilators are not available or unavailability of intensive care or high dependent unit care.", "runs": [{"text": "Consider oral vasodilators in case intravenous vasodilators are not available or unavailability of intensive care or high dependent "}, {"text": "unit care."}]}, {"id": "body-21512", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "C: isosorbide dinitrate (PO) 10–20mg 12hourly", "runs": [{"text": "C:", "bold": true}, {"text": " ", "bold": true}, {"text": "isosorbide"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "dinitrate"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(PO)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "10–20mg"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "12hourly"}]}, {"id": "body-21513", "type": "paragraph", "style": "Heading3", "text": "OR", "runs": [{"text": "OR"}], "level": 3, "conjunction": true}, {"id": "body-21514", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "B: hydralazine (PO) 25mg 6–8hourly. Maximum dose: 200 mg 24hourly Children", "runs": [{"text": "B:", "bold": true}, {"text": " ", "bold": true}, {"text": "hydralazine"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(PO)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "25mg"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "6–8hourly."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Maximum"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "dose:"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "200"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "mg"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "24hourly "}, {"text": "Children"}]}, {"id": "body-21515", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "PO: 0.75-3mg/kg/day divided 6-12 hours", "runs": [{"text": "PO:"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "0.75-3mg/kg/day"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "divided"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "6-12"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "hours"}]}, {"id": "body-21516", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "IV: 0.1-0.5 mg/kg/dose IV q6-8hr (maximum 2mg/kg/dose)", "runs": [{"text": "IV:"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "0.1-0.5"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "mg/kg/dose"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "IV"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "q6-8hr"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(maximum"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "2mg/kg/dose)"}]}, {"id": "body-21518", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Inotropes (Inotropic agents): Indicated in patients with hypotension (SBP <90 mmHg or mean arterial BP < 60mmHg) and peripheral hypoperfusion. Dosage see table 20.5 below.", "runs": [{"text": "Inotropes ", "bold": true}, {"text": "(Inotropic agents): Indicated in patients with hypotension (SBP <90 mmHg or mean arterial BP < 60mmHg) and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "peripheral hypoperfusion. Dosage see table 20.5 below."}]}, {"id": 